WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.078
Double-free defects often come from cleanup paths that evolved faster than the ownership model.

2
00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:09.561
A function may start with one allocation and one return.

3
00:00:09.561 --> 00:00:14.823
Later it gains more allocations, validation checks, early exits, and helper calls.

4
00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:22.235
If every branch grows its own cleanup logic, the same allocation can become eligible for release in more than one place.

5
00:00:22.235 --> 00:00:27.349
Centralized cleanup can reduce that risk when a function owns several resources.

6
00:00:27.349 --> 00:00:34.019
A single cleanup section can release only what was acquired, in a predictable order, and return one status.

7
00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:37.503
This is not a rule that every function must have one exit.

8
00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:43.654
It is a design option that makes release behavior easier to review when branching becomes complex.

9
00:00:43.654 --> 00:00:49.139
Cleanup should free only resources that were initialized and owned by the current code.

10
00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:58.181
Flags, null-initialized fields, construction state, and ownership transfer markers can all help cleanup logic decide what is safe to release.

11
00:00:58.181 --> 00:01:03.962
The important part is that the decision is based on tracked state, not on duplicated guesses.

12
00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:12.338
Avoid freeing the same member in several branches unless the state transition makes it impossible for another branch to free it again.

13
00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:18.860
Duplicated release logic should trigger a review question: can these paths both run for the same object?

14
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.084
If the answer is hard to prove, the structure needs simplification.

15
00:01:23.084 --> 00:01:27.013
Error handling should leave predictable state for the caller.

16
00:01:27.013 --> 00:01:32.571
An output pointer should be unchanged, null, or clearly owned according to the contract.

17
00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:38.278
A partially constructed object should not escape with ambiguous cleanup responsibility.

18
00:01:38.278 --> 00:01:42.429
A transferred object should not also be cleaned up by the old owner.

19
00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:46.653
Before adding a new return or failure case, review the cleanup flow.

20
00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:54.436
Identify what has been allocated, what has been transferred, what remains borrowed, and what must be released exactly once.

21
00:01:54.436 --> 00:01:59.475
Cleanup paths deserve the same engineering attention as successful execution.
